Yes. That is not normal. If the IP changes on every reboot, you'll continue to have this problem.

From your outputs, it looks like the node is getting a different IPv6 address after each reboot. OpenShift expects the node IP to remain stable.

The most likely causes are:

1. IPv6 is configured using SLAAC/privacy addresses instead of a stable address.


2. NetworkManager is regenerating the IPv6 address on each boot.


3. Your environment expects a static IPv6, but the network is assigning a new one after every reboot.



Verify whether IPv6 uses temporary addresses

Run:

sysctl net.ipv6.conf.all.use_tempaddr
sysctl net.ipv6.conf.ens3f0.use_tempaddr

and

nmcli connection show ens3f0 | egrep "ipv6.method|ipv6.addr|ipv6.token|ipv6.ip6-privacy"

Also check how the node IP is detected

From the bastion, run:

oc get network.config.openshift.io cluster -o yaml
oc get infrastructure cluster -o yaml

If this is a production SNO

The node should use a stable IP address. If IPv6 changes after every reboot, OpenShift can regenerate kubelet/CRI-O configuration with stale values, exactly as you observed.

Can you also tell me:

Is this a dual-stack (IPv4 + IPv6) cluster?

Is the IPv6 address configured statically, by DHCPv6, or by SLAAC/router advertisements?


That will determine the permanent fix.
